Hannah Arendt among the Cold War Liberals
Samuel Moyn
Of the innumerable interpretations of Hannah Arendt’s political thought, not one has placed her alongside the founding Cold War liberals—her contemporaries and, in later life, her companions—though doing so is indispensable and overdue.1 Such a comparison reveals that, like Isaiah Berlin, Karl Popper, Judith Shklar, Jacob Talmon, or Lionel Trilling, Arendt is best understood as displacing the intellectual premises of earlier liberalism, while foreclosing a global approach to the universalization of freedom and equality in an era of decolonization and deracialization. Generations of instrumentalizing, opportunistic, or promotional readings have obscured the relationship that Arendt sustained with the Cold War liberals. Restoring it to view helps reassess the contributions and limits of her project in political theory.
This article shows how Hannah Arendt is usefully reinterpreted in view of Cold War liberalism, the obverse goal from a lecture on her from a forthcoming book, which reinterprets Cold War liberals in light of her example. See Samuel Moyn, Liberalism against Itself: Cold War Intellectuals and the Making of Our Times (New Haven, CT: Yale University Press, 2023). Thanks to the editors of and referees for the Journal of the History of Ideas for help shifting agendas. 1 For a rare and technical exception that proves the rule, see Danielle Judith Zola Carr, “‘Ghastly Marionettes’ and the Political Metaphysics of Cognitive Liberalism: AntiBehaviourism, Language, and the Origins of Totalitarianism,” History of the Human Sciences 33 (2020): 147–74.
