Arendt wasn’t a liberal, she repeatedly declared—and she was therefore not a Cold War liberal.2 If anything, she initiated more direct conversations with conservative traditions: her esteem for Edmund Burke ran high, and for Friedrich von Gentz even higher.3 But even as she crafted her own idiosyncratic canon in the history of political thought, her very attempt to strike out on her own in developing a new account of freedom proved hostage to many of the intellectual and political premises of Cold War liberalism. A sketch of her biography resembles that of other Cold War liberals. Arendt was raised in Königsberg (where her family moved in 1909, three years after her birth ), only 250 miles from Berlin’s and Shklar’s Riga. And Arendt was famously a Jewish exile, as most Cold War liberals were. But such biographical commonalities were never determinative, certainly not compared to the controversial intellectual choices she and the Cold War liberals made.4 It is critical to my account of Arendt and Cold War liberalism alike that both departed from earlier liberalism. Nowadays there is far more agreement than ever before that nineteenth-century Continental Europe birthed liberalism, in a radically different guise from the twentieth-century Anglophone constructions that center on early modernity, personal liberty, and religious toleration.5 According to the recent literature, the founders of liberalism in the nineteenth century went on a rescue mission to integrate the emancipatory freedom and equality of the French Revolution with institutional stability and orderly progress.6 They were, in this regard, fellow travelers of G. W. F. Hegel, who in turn anticipated the spirit of and profoundly influenced much later liberal theory. The practice of nineteenth-century liberalism gravitated to free markets and parliamentary institutions.7 But the influence of the Romantic movement and certain forms of post-revolutionary
