age had eventuated in an abasing new form of rule called “totalitarianism.” Arendt’s The Origins of Totalitarianism expounded her version of that Cold War truism. It was in finalizing what had begun as a very different kind of book that Arendt vaulted herself from obscure journalist and solitary loner into the pantheon of Cold War critics of “totalitarianism.” In the fires of World War II, anti-totalitarianism had become central to liberal thinking.16 The pact between Adolf Hitler and Josef Stalin in in 1939, so disconcerting to antifascism, vindicated the originally center-right concept of “totalitarianism” that grouped illiberal regimes of left and right together—even though it would soon be undercut by a more left-leaning front against fascism. After 1945, the fragmentation of the alliance that had banded together to defeat the Axis revived anti-totalitarianism. Indeed, had the original formulations of the nature of totalitarianism not been routinized in the invention of Cold War liberalism, it is doubtful that the notion would have survived. Cold War liberalism breathed new spirit into the concept of totalitarianism, which otherwise might not have endured after World War II when fascism disappeared as a living political endeavor, a few old and new redoubts in Iberia and the Global South aside. Arendt joined in theorizing it—towering above all others in the prominence of her rendition. For all her originality, in other words, Arendt’s fame was parasitic on the crystallization of Cold War liberalism in the late 1940s. Arendt’s case raises the arresting irony that the thinker most enduringly associated with a concept engaged it accidentally and instrumentally. It is clear that when Arendt embarked in the United States on The Origins of Totalitarianism around 1946, with her background in existential philosophy and Jewish political journalism, it was solely intended as an autopsy of Nazi rule. “Although Arendt is known as one of the foremost proponents of the ‘totalitarian’ thesis,” Margaret Canovan observes, “totalitarianism in [the] sense [of theorizing the two regimes together] was not in fact the original subject of her book, and [its] last and most influential section was largely an afterthought.”17 Arendt set out to plumb the sources of Nazism not solely in antisemitism but also in imperialism, which were the only two topics Arendt initially planned for the book.
