Arendt’s plans changed with the crystallization of the Cold War in 1947–48.18 Shortly before the book’s appearance in 1951, she wrote an additional, third part of the book on “totalitarianism,” but its relation to the prior sections of her book as well as to earlier discourse on the subject remained unclear. She did not care to explain, and readers have long suspected that her rationale for adding the section on “totalitarianism” was largely opportunistic, an attempt to keep her original investigation of Nazism relevant in the guise of answering the concern about the Soviet Union that now prevailed. The British version of her book altered its famous American title to The Burden of Our Time, while the German version referred to “total domination” (totale Herrschaft) rather than “totalitarianism.”19 No matter: thanks to the smash hit she achieved with her topical book, Arendt was canonized as a pivotal thinker because she volunteered for the Cold War defense of freedom against the new form of rule that went beyond ancient typologies.
A DIFFERENT FREEDOM AND EQUALITY
Arendt’s writing after Origins of Totalitarianism made clear that, while she popularized Cold War liberal concern with the extremist state of the twentieth century, she nonetheless also drew on Romanticism that Cold War liberals wished to expunge from their own tradition. The Cold War liberals repudiated Romanticism almost entirely—frequently blaming it for totalitarianism itself. They therefore cut the tether to earlier liberalism unceremoniously. Arendt’s theory of political action, by contrast, was rooted in Romanticism even as she reconfigured it, making the sort of creative agency she idealized almost inaccessible in her very attempt to preserve it in an age of mass society. The sources of Arendt’s vision of political action as creative performance lay in late Romanticism, in Friedrich Nietzsche’s aestheticism, as well as in Nietzsche’s Weimar-era heirs.20 But where Romantic liberals had contem
