FREEDOM AGAINST THE FRENCH REVOLUTION
Although she preserved creative action as an evanescent goal of politics, Arendt chimed with Cold War liberals in omitting any traces of nineteenthcentury liberalism from their accounts of liberalism’s historical emergenceBerlin’s classic essay on Mill being the exception that proved the rule.28 And she fully shared their obsession with the trajectory from Rousseau, through the French Revolution, culminating in twentieth-century horror. Following her other constructive classic in political theory, The Human Condition (1958), Arendt’s On Revolution (1963) famously drew on ancient inspiration—but for the sake of defending the American Revolution’s Cold War freedom against the French Revolution’s derangements.29 In On Revolution Arendt covered Rousseau, the French Revolution, and Hegel by offering up what reads like a sophisticated rewrite of Talmon’s Cold War liberal classic Origins of Totalitarian Democracy (which had appeared the year after her own book on totalitarianism, and which she owned). In her book and wider thought, Arendt’s skepticism of Rousseau and Hegel, source and successor of the French Revolution, coincided with Cold War liberal scripts. One way of reading Arendt, in fact, is to observe her explicitly globalize her skepticism of the French Revolution’s legacy in a decolonizing age where Cold War liberals more regularly presented a Soviet or domestic threat as the most terrifying one.
