It was in the course of her unforgiving treatment of the French scene in On Revolution that Arendt sided most clearly with Cold War liberals. “Every man must have an attitude to the French Revolution,” English historian Herbert Butterfield remarked in 1949, “must make a decision about it somehow—as part of the stand that he generally takes in life.”30 Talmon was against it in part because he considered freedom from want as concession to a hedonism that led to totalitarianism. “Exasperated by famine and shortage,” he had written, “the masses confusedly and passionately clamoured that the Revolution should carry out its promises, that is to say, should make them happy.”31 Arendt was against it too—and, like some other Cold War liberals, in favor of the American Revolution. In her well-known critique of Arendt on the social question, political theorist Hanna Pitkin does not mention Talmon or explore the Cold War’s relevance.32 But it was absolutely essential to grasping how Arendt arrived at her notorious skepticism toward the social question. In some Canadian lectures, Butterfield—who was more conservative than Cold War liberals but also shared many of their assumptions—acknowledged that “fairer distribution of the world’s goods” followed necessarily from the intuition that “without social justice many people can possess nothing more than the empty shell of a purely formal liberty.” The trouble was the terrorism such a thought rationalized. “A whole civilization is being menaced if egalitarianism is turned into the absolute end.”33 Arendt, of course, agreed with such figures entirely. Far from viewing the economic realm as the condition of the possibility of credible freedom of any kind, Arendt looked on the modern economy in terms of the necessity that ancients had associated with the ancient oikos. It followed that any project of public freedom had to be non-economic; suggesting otherwise risked missing the point of political action, or even misleading it by substit