To begin with, Arendt’s construal of Atlantic revolution in the late eighteenth century—American and French—perpetuated the discourse back to Gentz of comparing some revolutions while leaving others out. It has been charged, by Kathryn Gines among others, that Arendt juxtaposed the American and French while excluding the Haitian Revolution from her account of modern bids for freedom—though C. L. R. James had already written Black Jacobins, the text that has inspired contemporary scholars to rectify that error, and that James revised for republication in the same year as Arendt’s On Revolution.54 But the deeper anxiety is not exclusion and silencing—itself almost universal in the anatomy of revolution for centuries—so much as Arendt’s clear assumption that non-white revolution would necessarily channel French manias. Had she included Haiti in On Revolution, Arendt would have found it pathological, merely worseningif such were possible—the French syndrome, by welding political freedom not just to class but also to racial equality. One doesn’t need to read between the lines to understand On Revolution as fundamentally about postcolonial derangement. The setting of the book, Arendt states clearly, was a time when “revolution has become one of the most common occurrences in the political life of nearly all countries and continents.”55 No doubt the legacy of 1789 ran through 1917. But in her own time the revolutionary legacy had passed to the Global South. It was in the postcolony that the choice between the American and the French/ Soviet models of revolution was live. Did Arendt even regard it as a choice? The French Revolution, establishing through Hegel the very notion of world politics, had already gone global. “The sad truth of the matter,” she wrote, “is that the French Revolution, which ended in disaster, has made world history, while the American Revolution, so triumphantly successful, has remained an event of little more than local importance.”56 And wasn’t it a