ZIONISM AS DECOLONIZATION
For Arendt as for the Cold War liberals, there was one main exception to the pessimism about or rejection of the prospects of global emancipationZionism.62 Like the other Cold War liberals, Arendt understood racism as a racialized Jewish victim of antisemitism. But their response to that racism was a kind of Jewish concession. Jews could strive violently for political freedom when other global peoples were not entitled to do the same. Once again, Arendt’s companionship with Cold War liberals forces a discomfiting perspective on her thought: like them, Arendt prized aspects of the collective and emancipatory legacy of prior liberalism for Jews but for no one else. Arendt’s embrace of popular and violent emancipation in her shortlived Zionism of the 1930s and early 1940s matched the comparable identifications of Cold War liberals, except that they did not follow her in abandoning Zionism in practice. Furthermore, lacking the desire Arendt preserved for emancipation in evanescent forms, the Cold War liberals tolerated a much more flagrant contradiction between their libertarian theories of freedom and their Zionist politics of collective emancipation. But like her, they allowed Jews in the Middle East a form of politics which was ruled out by the libertarian breviary developed for the West in the face of the Soviet enemy. Arendt’s political awakening had led her to embrace Zionism for a decade after 1933, prizing the activist (and even armed) political alternative it afforded Jews facing persecution. Then, with the Zionist choice in 1942–43 against the federalist Zionism she supported, Arendt dropped out of the movement. It has been noted how Arendt’s onetime enthusiasm for
