CONCLUSION
It was in part because Arendt did things other than encode Cold War liberalism that she has inspired a riot of successor projects, from accounts of participatory democracy to theories of republican freedom. C. L. R. James’s brilliant reinterpreter David Scott—while indicting Arendt’s erasure of Haiti—remarks, and rightly so, that “if in all the conventionally recognizable ways Arendt was a Eurocentric, this is not all that she was.”84 Arendt may have betrayed the aspiration to creative agency for individuals and groups of earlier liberalism grievously, but less grievously than
